{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And if these three he does not do for her.</b> If one of these three he does not do for her. And what are the three? He designates her for himself, or for his son, or he deducts from her redemption and she goes free — and this one he did not designate, neither to himself nor to his son, and she did not have the means to redeem herself." "<b>And she shall go out free.</b> Scripture makes her departure more expansive than what it makes for slaves. And what is the departure? It teaches you that she goes out by signs [of puberty] and remains with him further until she brings signs — and if six years arrive before signs, we have already learned that she goes out, as it says ״הָעִבְרִי אוֹ הָעִבְרִיָּה וַעֲבָדְךָ שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים״ (Deuteronomy 15). And what is meant here by ״וְיָצְאָה חִנָּם״? That if signs preceded the six years, she goes out by them. Or can you not say that she goes out except by physical maturity? Scripture says ״אֵין כָּסֶף״ to include departure by physical maturity; and if both of them were not stated, I would say ״וְיָצְאָה חִנָּם״ — this is physical maturity. Therefore both of them were stated, so as not to give the litigant an opening to object (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:33.442285273Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}